We are building a new open-source project tentatively called **OpenConsole**.

OpenConsole is inspired by the discontinued Teleconsole project but is a new implementation.

Its purpose is to let a user temporarily share a terminal with another person without exposing SSH, configuring port forwarding, or requiring inbound connectivity.

The host machine will run a CLI:

```
openconsole
```

The CLI will eventually:

1. Start or attach to a local PTY/shell.
2. Contact a configurable OpenConsole relay server.
3. Create a temporary session.
4. Establish an outbound encrypted WebSocket connection to the relay.
5. Display a temporary session identifier / URL.
6. Allow another user to interact with the terminal through the relay.
7. Destroy the session when the host exits.

Eventually users will be able to join through either:

```
ssh <session>@console.example.com
```

or a browser-based xterm.js terminal.

For the initial phases we will implement browser access first. Native SSH access will come later.

## Technology

Use:

* Go for the CLI.
* Go for the relay server.
* Go modules.
* TypeScript/Vite/xterm.js for the browser interface later.
* WebSockets for host-to-relay and browser-to-relay communication.
* Docker for server deployment later.

Do not introduce a database, Redis, Kubernetes, message queue, authentication provider, or other infrastructure unless explicitly requested.

The first versions should be intentionally simple and easy to self-host.

## Architectural goals

Maintain strict separation between:

* terminal/PTY handling
* session management
* protocol framing
* tunnel transport
* HTTP/WebSocket server

Do not tightly couple terminal data to WebSockets because alternative transports may be added in the future.

Create reusable Go packages wherever appropriate.

## Protocol

Create a small protocol abstraction that supports messages conceptually equivalent to:

* OPEN
* DATA
* RESIZE
* PING
* PONG
* CLOSE
* ERROR

Terminal payload data must be capable of being transferred as binary data without JSON/base64 encoding.

Control messages may use JSON.

Design the protocol so multiplexed logical channels can be added later for TCP forwarding, but DO NOT implement multiplexing yet.

Document the decisions in:

```
docs/protocol.md
```

## Repository layout

Create approximately:

```
cmd/
  openconsole/
  openconsole-server/

internal/
  client/
  server/
  session/
  tunnel/
  protocol/
  terminal/

docs/

deploy/

web/
```

Do not create empty abstractions merely to satisfy the directory structure. Only create packages that have an immediate purpose.

## Phase 1 objectives

For this phase ONLY:

1. Initialize the Go project.

2. Create the CLI executable skeleton.

3. Create the server executable skeleton.

4. Implement configuration parsing.

5. Define the protocol message types.

6. Implement secure random session ID generation.

7. Implement an in-memory SessionManager.

8. Build basic HTTP endpoints:

   ```
   GET /health
   POST /api/v1/sessions
   GET /api/v1/sessions/{id}
   ```

9. A created session should expire automatically after a configurable TTL.

10. Session IDs must be generated using crypto/rand, never math/rand.

11. Add structured logging.

12. Add unit tests for:

    * session ID generation
    * session creation
    * session expiration
    * lookup of nonexistent sessions

13. Create docs/architecture.md describing the planned system.

14. Create docs/protocol.md describing the initial protocol.

15. Create a useful README with local development instructions.

Do NOT yet implement:

* PTYs
* shells
* WebSockets
* terminal streaming
* browser UI
* SSH listener
* TCP forwarding
* persistent storage
* user accounts
* OAuth
* E2E encryption

We will implement these in subsequent phases.

## Security requirements

Treat all session identifiers and credentials as security-sensitive.

* Use crypto/rand.
* Never log secret tokens.
* Do not place authentication secrets into URLs unless specifically designed as a capability URL.
* Keep public session identifiers separate from secret credentials in the data model.
* Validate all externally supplied IDs.
* Set reasonable server timeouts.
* Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
* Never invoke a shell using untrusted request parameters.

For now, model sessions so that they can eventually contain both:

```
SessionID      // safe public identifier
HostToken      // credential used by host tunnel
GuestToken     // credential used to join
```

Tokens should be generated securely even though the tunnel is not implemented yet.

Store only what is needed in memory.

## Configuration

The server should support environment variables and command-line flags where reasonable, including:

```
OPENCONSOLE_LISTEN_ADDR
OPENCONSOLE_SESSION_TTL
OPENCONSOLE_LOG_LEVEL
```

Defaults:

```
listen: :8080
session TTL: 30 minutes
log level: info
```

The CLI should eventually support:

```
--server
```

but for this phase it may only display version/help information if appropriate.

## Development expectations

Write production-quality idiomatic Go.

Prefer the Go standard library where practical.

Avoid premature abstractions.

Return proper errors instead of panicking.

Pass context.Context through server operations where appropriate.

Use graceful HTTP server shutdown.

Use comments to explain architectural decisions rather than obvious code.

Run:

```
gofmt
go vet ./...
go test ./...
```

before considering the phase complete.

At the end, provide:

1. A summary of files created.
2. Important architectural decisions made.
3. Exact commands to build and run the server.
4. Example curl commands that prove session creation works.
5. Test results.
6. Any issues or decisions that should be addressed before Phase 2.

Do not proceed into Phase 2.

